  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Phasor Representation of a Cosine",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert a cosine A cos($\\omega$ t + $\\phi$) into the phasor A angle $\\phi$, preserving amplitude and phase for same-frequency phasor addition.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Phasor Representation of a Cosine",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Phasor Representation of a Cosine",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A phasor is the magnitude-angle version of a cosine. Use $A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi) \\mapsto A\\angle\\phi$ when the cosine will be handled through same-frequency phasor addition. The amplitude stays $A$, the phase shift becomes the angle, and the shared angular frequency $\\omega$ stays separate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A\\cos(\\omega t) \\rightarrow A\\angle 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The simplest base case is $A\\cos(\\omega t)$, which has zero phase. Its phasor is $A\\angle 0$, so a cosine with no phase shift becomes a phasor with angle $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi) \\rightarrow A\\angle\\phi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$, keep the amplitude unchanged and carry the phase shift into the phasor angle. The mapping preserves the information needed for later addition: the phasor stores the amplitude and phase, not the time variable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep $\\omega$ outside the phasor value. That separation matters later because same-frequency cosines are added only after they have the same $\\omega$, and the phasors then carry only the magnitude and angle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Phasor Representation of a Cosine",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Writing a Zero-Phase Cosine as a Phasor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
