          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a cosine with no phase shift: $A\\cos(\\omega t)$. Use the phasor convention that keeps the amplitude and converts the phase to an angle, so the base form is $A\\angle 0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A\\cos(\\omega t) \\rightarrow A\\angle 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For this example, the amplitude is $6$ and the phase shift is $0$, so the cosine $6\\cos(\\omega t)$ becomes the phasor $6\\angle 0$. Nothing changes except the notation: the time-varying cosine is now written as a magnitude-angle pair.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "6\\cos(\\omega t) \\rightarrow 6\\angle 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep the shared angular frequency $\\omega$ outside the phasor. The phasor stores only amplitude and phase, which is the form needed later when same-frequency terms are added.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
